  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "The number 1000 symbolizes completeness, infinity, divine abundance, and God's eternal faithfulness.",
      "description": "In the Bible, 1000 is often used to express an incomprehensibly large quantity or a perfect whole, rather than a literal number. In Psalm 50:10, God says that \"the cattle on a thousand hills\" belong to Him, expressing His infinite ownership over creation. In Revelation 20, the \"millennium\" is described as a period of Christ's perfect rule, and in Deuteronomy 7:9 it is promised that God keeps His covenant for \"a thousand generations,\" symbolizing His eternal and boundless faithfulness.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is well written and flows nicely, but it lacks footnotes that could enrich the text with interesting additional facts. For example, rabbinic interpretations of 'elef' (אלף) which can mean both 1000 and 'tribe/clan', kabbalistic symbolism, and the liturgical use of the number in Jewish tradition.; [minor] Footnotes should include information that the Hebrew word 'elef' (אלף) has multiple meanings, which is linguistically relevant and enriches the understanding of the number.; [minor] 2 Peter 3:8 ('For the Lord is one day as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day') is a very central verse for understanding 1000 in a biblical context and should be mentioned in the description.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew word for thousand, 'elef' (אלף), can also mean 'tribe', 'clan', or 'military unit'. This ambiguity has led to discussions among scholars about whether certain biblical numbers containing 'elef' should be understood as literal thousands or as group units.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In rabbinic tradition, the number 1000 is linked to the idea of a complete cycle in God's plan. Based on Psalm 90:4 and the interpretation of the days of creation, the notion developed that the world would exist for 6000 years followed by a thousand-year sabbath kingdom – a cosmic shabbat.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In Jewish liturgy and poetry (piyyut), \"thousand\" is used as hyperbolic praise of God, for example in expressions like \"a thousand tongues cannot grasp your greatness.\" This reflects the biblical use of the number to express what surpasses human comprehension.",
      "source": "liturgisk"
    }
  ]
}
